WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:06.380
Where I come from

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:08.210
we have a transatlantic way of seeing things

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:09.769
and we really don’t have any choice

00:00:09.769 --> 00:00:11.780
because it’s a transatlantic household.

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:12.780
My father is from Germany

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:15.030
and I grew up in Germany in a small town.

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:17.660
My mother is from Indiana in the United States
of America

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:19.490
also from a small town.

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:21.980
We used to go visit in summer every year

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:24.369
and I spent the summer with my cousins.

00:00:24.369 --> 00:00:25.920
And the would ask questions like

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.630
„Do you have electricity back in Germany?“

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:30.400
“Do you have TV back in Germany?”

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:32.040
You know, it was the ‘80s.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:35.230
It really showed me from a very early age
on

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:38.660
that you need to explain different realities.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:39.660
Look around here:

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:41.579
this is the heart of the European Union.

00:00:41.579 --> 00:00:44.070
We have 28 member states

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:45.750
and they all have their different reality

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:48.660
different languages, different cultures.

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:52.680
When leaders of the different EU countries
gather on nights like this,

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:54.870
then I think there is a real need

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:56.969
for journalists that people trust,

00:00:56.969 --> 00:00:59.170
that can make sense of all this

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:01.059
and can explain what’s actually happening
here.

00:01:01.059 --> 00:01:03.320
It’s sort of like coming full circle

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:05.220
when you look at what I did when I was young.

